          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Cordelia\",\"addressee\":\"Lear\",\"anchor_quote\":\"Unhappy that I am, I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth: I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love Your Majesty.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"Cordelia directly avows her love for Lear as a present emotional state. Her qualification measures the extent and basis of that love rather than displacing the avowal.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I am sure, my love’s\\nMore ponderous than my tongue.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":3,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"The occurrence expressly invokes Cordelia’s existing filial bond and affirms that her love is governed by it. The continuation identifies the associated duties as obedience, love, and honour. Thus it affirms and positions her within an existing parent-child obligation rather than creating a new promise or promising unlimited devotion.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I\\nReturn those duties back as are right fit,\\nObey you, love you, and most honour you.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.96,\"reason\":\"The expression is a controlled, deliberative answer in a public test, not a reflex-like affective discharge. Cordelia explicitly describes difficulty verbalising her heart and then gives a measured formulation.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Unhappy that I am, I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth: I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.86,\"reason\":\"Beyond avowing love and affirming a filial bond, the expression functions as a corrective refusal of Lear’s demand for competitive, expansive declarations. Its limiting formulation presents honest proportion—neither more nor less—in place of the rhetoric expected by the love test.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Which of you shall we say doth love us most?\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"According to my bond; no more nor less.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Why have my sisters husbands if they say\\nThey love you all?\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"In a public division of his kingdom, Lear asks his daughters to declare who loves him most, linking their answers to his bounty. Cordelia, speaking after her sisters’ expansive professions, answers Lear with a restrained avowal framed by her filial bond and rejects the premise that a married daughter should claim to give all love and duty to her father.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"Cordelia invokes and affirms an existing filial undertaking: to return the duties fittingly owed to the father who begot, raised, and loved her, including obedience, love, and honour. She does not make a new future promise or accept an unlimited or exclusive obligation; she expressly limits the bond to what is fitting and anticipates sharing love, care, and duty with a future husband. Lear does not accept this measured affirmation as adequate: he tells her to amend her speech and warns that it may damage her fortunes.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied passages are from William Shakespeare’s King Lear, including the opening love test and later reconciliation and final scenes.\",\"limitations\":\"The supplied evidence strongly supports the utterance’s avowal and bond-affirming force. It does not fully specify every legal, moral, or practical term of Cordelia’s filial obligation. Later passages support the reader’s assessment that her affection is genuine, but they do not convert the original utterance into a new promise or establish that Lear accepted her formulation.\"}"
        }
